{%- comment -%}
  ═══ LBASS — PRICE + CAMPAIGN STYLES (2026-09-09) ═══

  Rendered ONCE per document, never from inside a card. A card snippet runs up
  to 250 times on a collection page; a <style> block in there would ship 250
  copies of this sheet.

  MUST BE RENDERED AFTER snippets/lbass-stamp.liquid, for two reasons:
    1. It reuses that sheet's design tokens (--lb-ink-3, --lb-red, --lb-red-deep,
       --lb-bone). Every use below carries a literal fallback so the components
       still render correctly on templates/page.vault.liquid, which uses
       {% layout none %} and does NOT include lbass-stamp.
    2. That sheet carries 688 `!important` declarations and has already
       flattened two earlier components — see the removal note at
       templates/collection.liquid:174 and the anchoring note at
       layout/theme.liquid:860.

  The class names here (`lb-price__*`, `lb-promo-*`) collide with nothing in it,
  so ordering alone is enough. No `!important` is used or needed. Keep it so.

  ── WHY .lb-price__now CARRIES NO COLOUR OR FONT ─────────────────────────────
  On a collection card the price container also carries `.card-price`, which
  lbass-stamp:267 styles with the display face at 22px in --lb-ink, !important.
  That is the live card design. The current price inherits it untouched; only
  the NEW children below take their own type. A rule on a child element beats an
  inherited value from its parent, so none of this needs !important either.

  ── RTL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  The storefront renders `<html dir="rtl">`. Every offset here is logical
  (inset-inline-start, padding-inline, text-align:start), so the ribbon mirrors
  to the correct corner with no `[dir]` override anywhere.

  The price ROW is deliberately left-to-right: both existing sheets force
  `direction:ltr` on `.card-price`, and digits read LTR even in Arabic. The
  savings line sits outside that container and follows the card's RTL flow.

  ── MOBILE FIRST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Sized for the 2-column grid at 360-430px first; the desktop step only grows
  type. The ribbon is capped at the card width and wraps rather than overflowing.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
/* Ribbon and 1/1 stamp both anchor here. lbass-stamp sets this for `.grid .card`
   only, so declare it for every card context (search, rails, look cards). */
.card-img{position:relative}

/* ══════════ PRICE BLOCK ══════════ */
.lb-price{
  display:flex;
  flex-wrap:wrap;
  align-items:baseline;
  /* One consistent gap. The two prices must read as a pair, so the column gap
     is the distance between them at every breakpoint — no per-element margins
     drifting it. */
  gap:6px 9px;
}
/* Off-sale, .lb-price__now sets nothing — it inherits .card-price untouched.
   ON SALE it takes the lead: bigger than the struck price by a wide margin and
   heavier, so the number a shopper pays is the first thing the eye lands on.
   A rule on this child beats the inherited 22px/!important from the parent
   without needing !important of its own. */
.lb-price.is-sale .lb-price__now{
  /* Fluid rather than stepped, so the PAIR keeps fitting on one line as the
     column narrows. Measured: at 360px the card's price row is 130px wide and
     a fixed 21px/14px pair needs 137px — it wrapped, putting the reference
     price on a line of its own, which is the one layout a price comparison
     must not have. Scaling both together keeps them side by side and holds the
     ratio at 1.46-1.53x across every width. */
  font-size:clamp(19px,5.4vw,23px);
  line-height:1.05;
  letter-spacing:0;
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
  white-space:nowrap;
}
/* Colour is deliberately NOT changed on sale. The 1/1 stamp, the percentage
   pill and the savings line are already red on this card; a red price would be
   the fourth, which is what makes a card look cheap rather than premium. Size
   and weight carry the hierarchy instead. To make the sale price red anyway,
   add `color:var(--lb-red,#BF2E15)` here — one line, nothing else changes. */
/* THE REFERENCE PRICE. Its job is to be believed, not to be quiet, so it is
   secondary rather than faint.

   Moved from --lb-ink-3 (tertiary, 5.31:1 on the bone card) to --lb-ink-2,
   the palette's own "secondary text" token at 11.35:1 — more than twice the
   contrast, and semantically the right token: this IS secondary information,
   not tertiary. Weight 600 -> 700 for the same reason: a reference price that
   looks tentative reads as decoration rather than as a former price.

   The strikethrough itself is doubled to 2px. At 1px on a 14px numeral the
   line was thinner than the strokes it crossed, so it read as an artefact.
   `skip-ink:none` keeps it unbroken across every glyph.

   `text-underline-offset` was removed, not retuned: it moves UNDERLINES. It has
   never had any effect on a line-through and was doing nothing here.

   `nowrap` so "1 079 DH" can never break across two lines mid-number. */
.lb-price__was{
  font-family:var(--lb-body,inherit);
  font-size:clamp(13px,3.7vw,15px);
  font-weight:700;
  line-height:1.2;
  color:var(--lb-ink-2,#3A3A3C);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
  white-space:nowrap;
  text-decoration-thickness:2px;
  text-decoration-skip-ink:none;
}
/* The discount, second in the hierarchy behind the price itself.
   Was 11px red-on-10%-tint — SMALLER and softer than the 13-14px grey
   strikethrough it is supposed to outrank, so the eye reached the old price
   first. Now a solid red block: bigger than the strikethrough, still well under
   the 26px price, and legible at a glance rather than on inspection.
   Solid rather than merely larger, because a tint that size starts to look like
   a disabled control. */
.lb-price__off{
  font-family:var(--lb-body,inherit);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
  font-size:16px;
  font-weight:900;
  line-height:1;
  padding:5px 10px;
  border-radius:7px;
  color:var(--lb-bone,#F4EFE6);
  background:var(--lb-red,#BF2E15);
  border:1px solid var(--lb-red,#BF2E15);
}
.lb-price__save{
  margin:2px 0 0;
  font-family:var(--lb-body,inherit);
  font-size:11.5px;
  font-weight:800;
  line-height:1.35;
  letter-spacing:.01em;
  color:var(--lb-red,#BF2E15);
  /* PHYSICAL left, not `start`. This line belongs to the price block, and the
     price ROW is locked to `direction:ltr` by lbass-stamp, so it sits flush
     left even on the RTL storefront. With `text-align:start` the saving flushed
     RIGHT while the two prices above it flushed left — the block zig-zagged and
     stopped reading as one statement. Left matches the prices it describes.
     The Arabic word order inside the line is untouched: only the box moves. */
  text-align:left;
  unicode-bidi:isolate;
}

/* Announced-only label for the struck price and the percentage. `<s>` alone
   tells a screen reader nothing, so without this the two numbers are
   indistinguishable by ear. Also defined in lbass-collection-filters; the rules
   are identical, so either order is fine. */
.lb-sr{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}

/* ══════════ MARKDOWN BADGE (card image) ══════════ */
/* THIS piece's discount. Deliberately ink, not red, and on the opposite corner
   from the campaign ribbon — the two mean different things and must not read as
   one family. See snippets/lbass-markdown-badge.liquid. Square corners and the
   display face match the 1/1 stamp's vocabulary without copying its colour. */
.lb-md-badge{
  position:absolute;
  z-index:3;
  /* PHYSICAL bottom-right, in both directions, and this is deliberate.
     The image well already has two occupants and they do not agree:

       .lb-stamp   declares `direction:ltr` on ITSELF, so its
                   `inset-inline-start` resolves to LEFT whatever the page
                   does — it is pinned bottom-left and never mirrors.
       .lb-promo-ribbon  has no direction of its own, so it DOES mirror:
                   top-left in LTR, top-right in RTL.

     That leaves exactly one corner free in both directions: bottom-right.
     Measured: a logical `inset-inline-end` put this badge bottom-LEFT in RTL,
     directly on the stamp, and `inset-block-start` put it under the ribbon in
     LTR once the ribbon wrapped to two lines. Physical offsets are the only
     placement that collides with neither, and bottom-right sits immediately
     above the price block, where the eye is already heading. */
  bottom:10px;
  right:10px;
  display:inline-flex;
  align-items:center;
  justify-content:center;
  min-inline-size:58px;
  padding:7px 11px;
  border:1px solid var(--lb-ink,#141416);
  background:var(--lb-ink,#141416);
  color:var(--lb-bone,#F4EFE6);
  font-family:var(--lb-display,inherit);
  font-size:20px;
  font-weight:700;
  line-height:1;
  letter-spacing:.02em;
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
  pointer-events:none;
}
/* The numeral run is isolated with <bdi> in the markup rather than by declaring
   `direction:ltr` here. Same glyph order, but it leaves the box's axis alone —
   which is how the stamp ended up unable to mirror in the first place. */
/* A sold piece keeps its badge but stops shouting: the price is no longer an
   offer anyone can take, so the discount is information rather than promotion.
   It is also the only case where the badge does not fit. Measured at 360px:
   the image is 156px, the SOLD stamp is 75px against 52px for 1/1, and the
   full-size badge is 67px — 162px of content in 156px, so the two collide.
   Shrinking it here fixes the overlap exactly where it occurs, instead of
   shrinking the badge on every phone card to accommodate the one that is
   already unbuyable. */
.lb-sold .lb-md-badge{
  opacity:.45;
  font-size:14px;
  min-inline-size:44px;
  padding:5px 8px;
}

/* ══════════ CAMPAIGN RIBBON (card image) ══════════ */
.lb-promo-ribbon{
  position:absolute;
  inset-block-start:10px;
  inset-inline-start:10px;
  z-index:3;
  display:inline-flex;
  align-items:center;
  flex-wrap:wrap;
  gap:5px;
  max-inline-size:calc(100% - 20px);
  padding:5px 9px;
  background:var(--lb-red-deep,#8F1F0D);
  color:var(--lb-bone,#fff);
  font-family:var(--lb-body,inherit);
  font-size:9.5px;
  font-weight:800;
  line-height:1.25;
  letter-spacing:.04em;
  pointer-events:none;
}
.lb-promo-ribbon__campaign{text-transform:uppercase}
.lb-promo-ribbon__rate{
  font-size:11px;
  font-weight:900;
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
}
.lb-promo-ribbon__obj{opacity:.92}

/* ══════════ CAMPAIGN LINE (card body) ══════════ */
.lb-promo-line{
  display:flex;
  align-items:center;
  gap:5px;
  flex-wrap:wrap;
  margin:0;
  font-family:var(--lb-body,inherit);
  font-size:11px;
  font-weight:800;
  line-height:1.35;
  color:var(--lb-red-deep,#8F1F0D);
  text-align:start;
}
.lb-promo-line__rate{
  padding:2px 6px;
  border-radius:999px;
  background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--lb-red,#BF2E15) 10%,transparent);
  border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--lb-red,#BF2E15) 28%,transparent);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
}

/* ══════════ CAMPAIGN BLOCK (product page) ══════════ */
/* The PDP is the dark palette, so this block uses the gold tokens, not bone. */
.lb-promo-pdp{
  margin:14px 0 0;
  padding:12px 14px;
  border-radius:12px;
  background:rgba(200,154,14,.07);
  border:1px solid rgba(200,154,14,.26);
  text-align:start;
}
.lb-promo-pdp__head{
  display:flex;
  align-items:center;
  flex-wrap:wrap;
  gap:8px;
  margin:0;
  font-size:14px;
  font-weight:900;
  color:var(--gold2,#E0B52A);
}
.lb-promo-pdp__campaign{text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.03em}
.lb-promo-pdp__rate{
  padding:3px 8px;
  border-radius:999px;
  background:rgba(200,154,14,.16);
  border:1px solid rgba(200,154,14,.34);
  color:var(--gold,#C89A0E);
  font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;
}
.lb-promo-pdp__body{
  margin:7px 0 0;
  font-size:13px;
  font-weight:700;
  line-height:1.6;
  color:var(--txt2,#97928A);
}
.lb-promo-pdp__note{
  margin:5px 0 0;
  font-size:12px;
  font-weight:600;
  line-height:1.6;
  color:var(--txt3,#5E5A55);
}

/* Under 750px lbass-stamp drops .card-price to 19px, so the on-sale size steps
   down with it and keeps the same ratio against the struck price. Explicitly
   restated rather than left to inherit, because the parent rule is !important
   and the ratio is the whole point of the hierarchy. */
@media(max-width:749px){

  .lb-md-badge{
    bottom:8px;
    right:8px;
    min-inline-size:50px;
    padding:6px 9px;
    font-size:17.5px;
  }
}
/* Very narrow phones: the 2-column grid leaves ~150px of card width, and the
   price row plus a struck price plus a pill will wrap. Let it wrap, but keep
   the sale price on its own first line so it is never the thing that gets
   pushed below the fold. */
/* Narrowest phones. The sale price used to take `flex:1 0 100%` here, which
   forced it onto a line of its own and pushed the reference price BELOW it —
   the one layout that makes the comparison hard to read, and the opposite of
   what a price pair is for. Measured at 360px the pair needs ~112px of a ~140px
   content box, so they fit side by side and the discount pill wraps instead. */
@media(max-width:400px){
  .lb-price{gap:5px 6px}
  .lb-price__off{padding:4px 8px;font-size:15px}
}
@media(min-width:750px){
  /* Past the phone range the column stops being the constraint, so both settle
     at fixed sizes rather than tracking the viewport. */
  .lb-price.is-sale .lb-price__now{font-size:26px}
  .lb-price__was{font-size:17px}
  /* The pill steps up WITH the reference price, not independently. Raising the
     reference price to reach the requested 1.5x ratio would otherwise push it
     past the discount and invert `price > discount > reference`. Ties are what
     measure_sale.js caught last time: 14px against 14px is not a hierarchy. */
  .lb-price__off{font-size:18px;padding:6px 12px}
  .lb-price__save{font-size:12px}
  .lb-promo-ribbon{font-size:10px;padding:6px 10px}
  .lb-promo-ribbon__rate{font-size:12px}
  .lb-promo-line{font-size:11.5px}
}
</style>
